{
  "beruf": "elektro-technik",
  "title": "Agriculture and farm work",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Many newcomers start work in the campagna: tomatoes in Puglia and Campania, greenhouses in Sicily and Lazio, the vendemmia in Piedmont and Veneto, olives in the south, fruit in Emilia-Romagna and Trentino. This deck has tools and machines, the jobs in the fields, the season, pay and the contract, safety in the heat and with treatment products, and how to talk with the team. It also explains the caporalato: what it is, that it is illegal, and where to ask for help. In a squadra of colleagues, nearly everyone says tu; with the titolare, use Lei until he or she says otherwise.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work on a farm or in a greenhouse in Italy? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Bracciante agricolo",
    "Operaio agricolo",
    "Raccoglitore/raccoglitrice",
    "Addetto/a alla serra",
    "Trattorista",
    "Addetto/a al magazzino della frutta"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Ask on the first day how you are paid: per hour, per day (a giornata) or per cassetta (a cottimo). Write down your days and your cassette every evening, because you may need them later.",
    "In the summer, start early, drink water often and rest in the shade. If a colleague feels dizzy, is very hot or stops making sense, call 112. Many regions issue summer heat rules that stop field work in the hottest hours (to verify for your region).",
    "Do not go into a field or a greenhouse that has just been treated. Ask: «Si può rientrare?» and read the label or the notice.",
    "Farm contracts follow the national collective agreement (CCNL operai agricoli) and a provincial agreement, and they can differ by province and by job. Ask the employer, a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro; this is general orientation, not legal advice.",
    "Caporalato is illegal. Warning signs: someone else chooses and pays you, you pay for the transport or a place to sleep, you have no contract or payslip, your documents are kept, the pay is far below the contract. You can ask a union, the patronato or the Ispettorato del lavoro for help, and call 112 if you are in danger (details to verify)."
  ],
